No first-version operation produces a single physical unit referenced by multiple pointers at different offsets. Reflink and dedup share entire units (extent_off=0 for all pointers), not partials. Partial writes trigger whole-unit RMW to a new location (F2), so old units remain intact but only referenced by snapshots with extent_off=0. RAID rebuild, journal replay, snapshot delete, and restripe similarly handle whole units. Tombstone packing (D18) reuses entire units, not partials. Compression isn't in v1 (F5), so no sub-unit packing. All operations either create new units or share entire units with uniform offset. Thus, no splitting (extent_off≠0) occurs in v1. The "reserved but disabled" stance aligns with F9's fixed-width reservation and F5's compression exclusion. D9 item 4's reasoning holds: extent_off=0 today, and if enabled later, it still must not be in AAD for the same verification reasons. Reserved bits are justified per F9's "reserve, don't grow later" rule.
